A Beginner's Guide to GUNZ 3.26.0
What is GUNZ?

GUNZ (Graduated Underlying Navigation Zones) is a deterministic tool for finding the best operating frequencies of any system that advances in fixed clock steps. Think of a game engine ticking 4,800 times per second, a hardware timer, a motor controller, a network packet scheduler, or any process where time is measured in whole numbers of a base unit (nanoseconds, microseconds, milliseconds, etc.). GUNZ scans a range of frequencies, simulates exactly how many ticks are needed to reach a target, and produces a compact resonance map that tells you where the timing is stable, where it jitters, and which frequencies to avoid.

The name "Graduated" reflects the evolution of the project: what began as a tool for a single broken game engine has matured into a general-purpose, interdisciplinary standard for discrete-clock system analysis. The "zones" are the frequency intervals you explore; the "navigation" is the AI-driven or human-driven search for stable operating points; the "underlying" acknowledges that the physics of the clock is the foundation of everything.
Why use GUNZ?

    It is deterministic. The same inputs always produce exactly the same map, on any machine, in any programming language, on any operating system. Share a scan with confidence—the map is the truth, not an approximation.

    It works at any time scale. From seconds to picoseconds, just set the time_unit (0=seconds, 3=milliseconds, 6=microseconds, 9=nanoseconds, 12=picoseconds). The arithmetic scales cleanly because everything is expressed in the base time unit you choose.

    It is built for automation. Deterministic output and companion metadata (.gunzmeta) let AI agents compare scans, adjust parameters, and resume work without human help. The session_id field lets you group related scans; the chain_next_fingerprint and chain_sequence fields let you build temporal chains of maps, creating a persistent memory of exploration.

    It stores everything needed to reproduce a scan. Metadata captures the exact parameters, zones, scan cost, and even the provenance of the reference_block_time (explicit, guessed, calibrated, or overflow-clamped). You (or an AI) can re-run the same analysis years later and get bit-identical results.

    It is a truth-seeking compass. Each scan is a checkpoint. If a new configuration makes things worse, you can reload the last good map (using the fingerprint chain) and try a different path. Over many iterations, each map becomes the anchor for the next.

What does GUNZ produce?

When GUNZ scans a frequency range, it creates:

    A .gunzmap file – a small binary table (16 bytes per entry) that maps every frequency step to its:

        block_time_inaccuracy – the absolute difference between the measured block time and the ideal reference time. (This field was renamed from block_time_delta in 3.23.0 to better reflect that it's an absolute deviation, not a signed difference.)

        jitter – how much the block time wobbles when you nudge the frequency slightly (the classic timing jitter).

        advance_regularity – the consistency of per-cycle advance values inside a block. This metric was renamed from motion_jitter in 3.24.0 to better reflect that it measures regularity, not just "motion." Low advance regularity means the per-cycle timing varies; high advance regularity means it's consistent.

        peak_advance_regularity – the worst-case advance regularity across the jitter window.

        stability_width – the width of the stable region around the sweet spot.

        flags – STAB_SATURATED (stability width exceeded 6.5535 Hz), FLAG_AVOID (detrimental resonance), FLAG_SKIPPED (not evaluated), or FLAG_LONG_TERM (aggregated over multiple blocks).

    A .gunzmeta file – a human‑readable JSON sidecar with scan settings, timestamps, fingerprint, and a quality summary. It records the exact plan_parameters, scan_zones, scan_cost, and optionally resonance_quality. The .gunzmeta file is the canonical-status carrier: it tells you whether the map is canonical (trustworthy) or non-canonical (experimental, approximate, or unsandboxed).

    Optionally, a .gunzstats or .gunzstatz file – raw statistics for long‑term measurements that can be chained across hours or days without losing precision. These are used when long_term_scan is enabled.

How do I use GUNZ?
1. Define Your System

Choose the parameters that describe your discrete-clock system:
Parameter	What it means	Example (GunZ game engine)
quantum	The smallest time step (in base time units).	1,000,000 (1 ms, since timeGetTime() has 1ms granularity, and we work in nanoseconds — time_unit = 9)
target_ticks	The number of ticks you need to reach (the "finish line").	1140 (the animation block length)
mult_num / mult_den	The advance multiplier (e.g., ticks per quantum).	mult_num=48, mult_den=10 (4.8 ticks per ms)
round_mode	How to round fractional ticks.	1 (half-to-even, the default)
hold_cycles / sep_cycles	Extra cycles after the target.	0 (none)
time_unit	The base time scale.	9 (nanoseconds)
reference_block_time	The ideal block time you expect (the "truth anchor").	248,000,000 (248 ms in nanoseconds)
jitter_weight	How much to penalise timing jitter.	4 (default)
advance_regularity_weight	How much to penalise low advance regularity.	1 (neutral, default)
jitter_window_radius	The radius of the jitter window.	10000 (21 points, ±0.1 Hz)

Note: The advance_regularity_weight was added in 3.24.0, replacing the dead ppm_drift_scalar field. The reference_source field (0=explicit, 1=guessed, 2=calibrated, 3=overflow-clamped explicit) records the provenance of your reference_block_time.

Important: The quantum value must be expressed in the same base time unit as time_unit. For example, if time_unit = 9 (nanoseconds), a 1 ms clock granularity is quantum = 1,000,000. If you prefer to keep quantum = 1, you must set time_unit = 3 (milliseconds) and express all values in milliseconds.
2. Choose a Frequency Range

Decide which frequency interval to scan and how finely (the step size). A "zone" is just a start‑to‑end frequency range in 10⁻⁵ Hz units.

    min_freq = 20000000 (200.00000 Hz)

    max_freq = 30000000 (300.00000 Hz)

    step_freq = 1 (the default, 10⁻⁵ Hz resolution)

You can also use SCAN_RES to set a different step size, or SKIP_ZONE to skip evaluating a zone entirely (useful for gaps you already know are bad).
3. Run the Scan

A GUNZ runtime processes the zones and writes the map and metadata files.

    Minimal Profile: You can build a tiny, fully conformant scanner that only handles static zones and spits out a valid .gunzmap. No bytecode VM, no callbacks, no sandboxing required. This is the recommended starting point for embedded systems or fixed-range sweeps.

    Full Profile: For AI-driven exploration, you can use the bytecode VM, dynamic opcodes, and callbacks (PURE_CALL) to generate scan programs dynamically.

(The GUNZ 3.26.0 specification is complete. A reference runtime and conformance test suite are under development. You can start with a minimal implementation—just the static lane, a zone evaluator, and map output—and be fully conformant.)
4. Read the Map

The map shows which frequencies are sweet spots (low jitter, stable), which are detrimental (high jitter, flagged with FLAG_AVOID), and where there are large gaps (emitted as long‑jump entries).

    Sweet spots: Low block_time_inaccuracy, low jitter, and wide stability_width.

    Detrimental zones: High jitter or high advance_regularity converted to base time units exceeding the thresholds.

    Long‑jump entries: Gaps larger than ±0.32767 Hz are compressed into a single entry with run_length == 0 and block_time_inaccuracy == 0, storing the gap size in bytes 8–15 of the entry (re-interpreted as a little-endian s64).

5. Adjust and Repeat

Use time_unit to coarsen or refine the scan, tune the jitter window radius or jitter weight, and chain multiple maps with fingerprint links.

    Coarse scans (large time_unit, e.g., 3 = milliseconds) are fast and reveal major edges.

    Fine scans (small time_unit, e.g., 9 = nanoseconds) are more accurate but more expensive.

    Adaptive multi-resolution scanning is a core design pattern: start coarse, deepen when silent, ascend when costly, verify upward, and re-probe if no edge appears.

Each scan is a checkpoint. The .gunzmeta records exactly which truth anchor and scan parameters were used, and can optionally link to the previous checkpoint via a fingerprint chain (chain_next_fingerprint and chain_sequence). This lets you explore without fear—if a new configuration makes things worse, reload the last good checkpoint (using the fingerprint link) and try a different path. Over many iterations, each map becomes the anchor for the next, and GUNZ turns from a one‑time scanner into a truth‑seeking compass.








